parts.text:
`express.json({ limit: '50mb' })` globally — fine, but I'll still cap the app list payload explicitly (e.g. 400 apps, icon ≤ 64KB each) to avoid a runaway cache file.

hubTmp isn't used in server.js. I'll require it: `const hubTmp = require('./storage/hub-tmp');`

Now let me write the server code.

Placement: I'll put the API section right after the ADB block (line ~699) since it's device-related, and the page route with the other page routes.

Let me write it.

```js
// ─── LAUNCHER (the phone's own apps; no runner involved) ──────────────
// A launcher needs the phone's package list, and only the phone can answer it:
// inside the panel APK's WebView the native ZenBridge.listApps() does, and the
// page pushes what it saw to /api/apps so a plain browser on the same phone
// has something to draw offline too. The hub only keeps that report on disk —
// it never asks a runner, a workflow or ADB for the list.
const APPS_CACHE = path.join(hubTmp(), 'phone-apps.json');
const APPS_MAX = 400;
const APPS_ICON_MAX = 96 * 1024;

function readAppsCache() {
  try {
    const raw = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(APPS_CACHE, 'utf8'));
    const apps = Array.isArray(raw.apps) ? raw.apps : [];
    return {
      success: true,
      updatedAt: raw.updatedAt || 0,
      device: raw.device || '',
      source: raw.source || '',
      count: apps.length,
      apps
    };
  } catch {
    return { success: true, updatedAt: 0, device: '', source: '', count: 0, apps: [] };
  }
}
```
Wait, `apps` may be big; sending it on every GET is fine (few hundred KB, and offline.js caches GETs).

POST validation:
```js
app.post('/api/apps', express.json({ limit: '8mb' }), (req, res) => {
  const body = req.body || {};
  const src = Array.isArray(body.apps) ? body.apps : null;
  if (!src) return res.status(400).json({ success: false, error: 'нужен список apps' });
  const seen = new Set();
  const apps = [];
  for (const a of src.slice(0, APPS_MAX)) {
    const pkg = String((a && a.pkg) || '').trim();
    if (!pkg || seen.has(pkg)) continue;
    // Метка от телефона: без неё в списке было бы только имя пакета.
    const label = String((a && a.label) || pkg).trim().slice(0, 120) || pkg;
    const icon = typeof (a && a.icon) === 'string' && a.icon.length <= APPS_ICON_MAX ? a.icon : '';
    seen.add(pkg);
    apps.push(icon ? { label, pkg, icon } : { label, pkg });
  }
  const payload = {
    updatedAt: Date.now(),
    device: String(body.device || '').slice(0, 80),
    source: String(body.source || '').slice(0, 40),
    apps
  };
  try {
    fs.writeFileSync(APPS_CACHE, JSON.stringify(payload));
  } catch (e) {
    return res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: e.message });
  }
  res.json({ success: true, count: apps.length, updatedAt: payload.updatedAt });
});
```

`fs` — is it required in server.js? Let me check. Probably yes. I'll verify.

Now the page. `apps.html` following git.html's shape:

```html
<body data-page="apps">
  <div class="topbar" id="topbar"></div>
  <div class="main">
    <div class="content">
      <div class="page on">
        <div class="ph">
          <h1>📲 Лаунчер</h1>
          <p>Приложения этого телефона. Список и запуск — с самого устройства: панель-APK отдаёт его нативно, браузер открывает через <code>intent://</code>. Раннер не участвует, сеть не нужна.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card" id="lc-gate"> ... button «Включить лаунчер» ... </div>
        <div id="lc-status"></div>
        <div id="lc-tools" style="display:none"> search + refresh + count </div>
        <div id="lc-grid" class="apps-grid"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <script src="offline.js"></script>
  <script src="bridge.js"></script>
  <script src="apps-app.js"></script>
</body>
```

Need CSS for the app grid — add to hub.css (shared) as `.apps-grid`, `.app-tile`, `.app-ico`, etc. Since hub.css is the shared stylesheet, adding there is right (and it's already in the SW shell).

`apps-app.js`:

```js
/* apps-app.js — лаунчер телефона. Локальная штука: раннер here не участвует. */
'use strict';

const bridge = window.ZenBridge || null;   // есть только внутри WebView панели
let ALL = [];
let MODE = 'off';   // off | native | cached
```

Detection of native:
```js
function nativeBridge() {
  const b = window.ZenBridge;
  return b && typeof b.listApps === 'function' && typeof b.launchApp === 'function' ? b : null;
}
```
Note: `addJavascriptInterface` methods are accessible; but calls must be wrapped in try/catch (like `appsLoad()` in the panel does).

Also important: Android @JavascriptInterface calls are synchronous and run on a background thread — the panel calls them synchronously (`JSON.parse(ZenBridge.listApps())`). So same pattern is fine.

Enable flow:
```js
async function lcEnable() {
  const b = nativeBridge();
  if (b) { load native; mode native; }
  else { load cached from /api/apps; if none -> message; mode cached }
  render
  if (native) push to hub (best effort)
}
```

Launch:
```js
function lcOpen(pkg) {
  const b = nativeBridge();
  if (b && MODE === 'native') { try { b.launchApp(pkg); return; } catch (e) {} }
  // В браузере телефон сам разрулит intent — это единственный способ запустить
  // приложение из веб-страницы, и он работает без всякого сервера.
  location.href = 'intent://#Intent;package=' + encodeURIComponent(pkg) + ';end';
}
```
Hmm — `location.href = 'intent://...'` — Chrome requires `S.browser_fallback_url` sometimes; without it, if no app resolves, nothing happens (silent). Add a fallback: after 1.2s, if the document is still visible, offer `market://details?id=pkg`. That's a nice touch but adds complexity + timers. Let me include a simple fallback: set a timer that, if `document.visibilityState === 'visible'`, shows a small hint with a Play Store link. Actually simpler and honest: keep the hint text in the UI ("если не открылось — ищите в Play Market") and provide a per-tile long-press? No, keep it simple: no timer. Hmm.

Actually, let me do the fallback properly but cheaply: a single timer per launch that shows a toast "Не открылось? Открыть в Play Market" with the market link, auto-hiding. It's ~10 lines and makes the browser mode not feel broken. I'll implement it.

Search: filter by label/pkg, `oninput`.

Icons: `data:image/png;base64,...` from the pushed/native list; fallback to a letter tile.

Also: "работает даже без сети" — the page should work when the hub is unreachable? No: the page itself comes from the hub, so if the hub is down you can't load it — unless the SW cached it (which is why /apps goes in SHELL). Within the panel WebView the native mode needs no network at all. Good.

Also the page should auto-enable when `ZenBridge` is present (no click needed) — but the user explicitly wants a *button* «включить лаунчер». Compromise: show the button always; if native bridge is detected, the button is already highlighted and one tap activates. Auto-activate on load if native? The user wants the button — keep the button as the explicit action, but pre-fill the status line with "панель на телефоне — список возьмём отсюда" so it's one tap. I'll make the button the single explicit action (matches the request), and ALSO auto-load in native mode on load — hmm, that contradicts "кнопка включить лаунчер".

Decision: the button is the switch. Nothing loads until pressed (predictable, no surprise network calls). Status line tells you what will happen. This matches the request literally.

Wait — one important consideration: should the launcher page, when in native mode, push to `/api/apps`? The user wants the hub to hold the launcher. Pushing is what makes browser mode work. But it sends the phone's app list to the hub (which may live on a runner/PC). The user said the launcher shouldn't relate to the runner — pushing data to the hub is exactly "in the same hub", so it's consistent with "находиться в том же хабе". But is it needed? For the panel-WebView mode, no. For browser mode, yes (otherwise browser mode has no list at all).

I'll implement the push but make it explicit and opt-out-able: a checkbox "отдать список хабу (чтобы браузер тоже видел)" default ON, since the user asked for it to live in the hub. Hmm, extra UI. Simpler: always push in native mode, and state it in the note. The user asked for the launcher in the hub; sharing the list with the hub is the point. But privacy-wise: it's the user's own hub. Fine — always push, documented in the note.

